namespace Eling.Backend.Mcp;

/// <summary>
/// Static text delivered to MCP clients during the initialize handshake.
/// </summary>
public static class ServerInstructions
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Individual instruction sections broken down by semantic responsibility.
    /// </summary>
    public static readonly string[] Sections =
    [
        "Eling is a durable markdown-backed memory system. Markdown files under '.eling/memories/' are the canonical source of truth and are NOT tracked in git: leave '.eling/memories/' ignored, and never commit a memory file - or start tracking them - unless the user explicitly asks. All memory operations go through Eling MCP tools; never read, search, or write those files directly. An 'Eling <something>' prompt is a recall instruction: retrieve what was previously stored.",

        "Repo hygiene: generated runtime files MUST be gitignored - logs, '.eling/runtime/', and every SQLite database plus sidecars ('*.db', '*.db-*'). In a project workspace, check '.gitignore' covers these; if a runtime file is not ignored yet, confirm with the user before touching '.gitignore'.",

        "Recall (MUST DO): call `memory_recall` BEFORE any response or tool call on the first turn of a new session, with a BROAD topic set - the opening message's subject plus standing rules, preferences, decisions, lessons, gotchas, workflow conventions, and recently touched work; never anchor only on the opening subject. Repeat it on every topic shift or plan-step transition, before git commit, and when debugging recurring errors. Skip recall on casual chat and repeated micro-steps. Check `stats.recallCount`; if 0, re-issue with broader terms before proceeding. Every recall also returns `standingMemories` (a topic-independent baseline) - read it, and read `recallSummary` when present so overflow is never skipped.",

        "Save (MUST DO): when the turn contains something meant to outlive this conversation, call `memory_save` BEFORE replying. Tier 1 - what the user states: a standing rule or preference, a correction, a decision and its rationale, an explicit 'remember this', or a restatement of a durable rule (repetition is itself the signal). Tier 2 - what you observe: a project or environment fact NOT derivable from the tree (a gotcha, a tooling quirk, a disproven assumption, where something actually lives). Never let a plain acknowledgement ('okay', 'noted', 'siap', 'sure') substitute for the call. Skip transient task state, speculation, unverified claims, and anything the repo already records. At each phase transition, and once before the session ends, ask: what here would let a future session or a different agent avoid rediscovering it - a verified command and its exact invocation, an environment quirk and its workaround, a decision and why, a ruled-out dead end. Save each as a standalone fact a stranger can apply, never a transcript. Map the tier to `type`: preference / lesson / decision / fact.",

        "Scope - you choose it, never ask the user: climb by where the knowledge stays TRUE. (1) true only in this repo (decisions, conventions, repo-specific lessons, in-progress or scratch state, machine notes) -> project-local (default, machine-only, never committed, shared across this repo's worktrees). (2) true in any project (general coding knowledge, cross-project preferences, tooling rules, lessons about your own behavior) -> global. Boundary question: would this still be correct in a repo that has never heard of this one? Yes -> global, no -> project-local. Hoarding repo-specific facts into global is the costlier error. Use scope=project (committed, shared via git) ONLY when the user explicitly wants this memory shared for this repo; project-local outranks project in every tie. Content is written in English by default, tags are lowercase English, and memories stay portable - no absolute paths or usernames. `project-local` and `global` saves never need initialization; the committed project tier requires explicit user consent, checked via the recall's `projectScope.adoptable` or `memory_project_status` (ask before `memory_init_project`; on decline, route to project-local).",

        "Codebase: Eling keeps a per-workspace FTS index of the working directory so agents search code without a terminal. On the first turn, read the `codebase` field attached to the `memory_recall` response before any code search. If no index exists (fileCount 0), ask the user before running `codebase_index`; if stale, ask before refreshing - unless a linked git worktree of an already-indexed repo is building its own startup pass, which needs no approval. Search order: `codebase_search` (concept terms, narrowed with pathPrefix/filePattern, read only the returned line ranges) first; native grep/glob/shell search only when it returned 0 hits, the query needs matching FTS cannot express, or the index is unavailable/stale - and name which reason. The index is a rebuildable cache; deleting it loses nothing.",

        "Files: prefer Eling's sandboxed file tools over host-native equivalents inside the workspace; resolve paths against `workspace_root`. Host-native tools and read-only external access remain acceptable fallbacks. `file_write` is for NEW files; modify existing files with `file_edit`.",

        "Databases & Redis: SQL tools (db_list_connections, db_get_schema, db_query, db_detect_connections, db_import_connections) plus redis_scan/redis_inspect. db_query is read-only (SELECT/EXPLAIN/PRAGMA/SHOW/DESCRIBE/DESC/WITH); Redis entries are rejected by db_get_schema/db_query. A connection registered without a database is server-level. No credentials ever appear in parameters, outputs, or error messages. Import only with explicit approval - detect first, show each unimported candidate (settingsKey, derived name, source file), then import exactly the approved keys; never auto-import. `fromCache=true` means the answer came from a stale cache: re-read once the database is back.",

        "Diagnostics: use `log_list`/`log_read` for the rolling daily logs (backend / desktop streams; level, substring, and tail filters) rather than reading .eling log paths directly.",

        "`shell_execute` runs a shell command in the workspace. It is disabled unless enabled in `<user-scope>/config/shell-config.json` (or the dashboard Shell page); when disabled it returns `{ ok: false, code: \"shell_disabled\" }`. Enabling it is RCE-equivalent: restrict paths and rules deliberately.",

        "Commit lease: other chats on this checkout and branch share one git index, so a commit made at the wrong moment sweeps another chat's staged work into it. The `commit_*` tools protect the window between the first staging and the landed commit. Before `git add`, call `commit_check_conflict` (session_id, paths[]). Red means do not stage: the red cases are a live foreign Commit lease on this checkout+branch (holder info - session, lease age, intent - is in the response) or live foreign File leases on the requested paths; anything else is green. `staged_without_lease: true` is a warning, not a hard error. On red, either call `commit_wait` (server-side blocking wait with a timeout; note it does NOT take the slot - after it returns green you must still call `commit_lease`) or stop and report to the user (holder info plus \"retry later or wait for the lease to release\"), and do not stage. `commit_lease` requires `branch` - the current branch, e.g. from `git branch --show-current`; it does not default to anything. On green, must call `commit_lease` BEFORE the first staging, and must call `commit_release` immediately after the commit lands. The lease expires 300s after it is taken and there is no renewal tool yet (heartbeat ships with the unimplemented session-context phase; until it ships there is no MCP heartbeat call), so long-running commits should retake the lease or keep releases promptly - or at minimum be aware of the TTL. This protects against other chats on the same checkout+branch sweeping each other's staged work into one commit; do not bypass it with `git commit -- <own files only>` workarounds - the slot exists for a reason.",
    ];

    /// <summary>
    /// Merged instruction text joined by double newlines for clear paragraph separation.
    /// </summary>
    public static readonly string Default = string.Join("\n\n", Sections);
}
